Chapter: UefiReadKeyNoFirmware

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 BACKLOG 7.17. Every uefi-* helper used to read the UEFI system table pointer
 from GPA 0x8000. That address is the PML4: the boot code builds the page
 tables there and loads CR3 with it. So a helper read page-table entry zero,
 dereferenced it as a system table, took a function pointer out of the result
 and CALLED it -- an invalid-opcode fault at a low address, on every boot, in
 every mode. It could never have worked, and it went unfound for months
 because an app has to COMPILE before anyone can discover that its keyboard
 is fatal, and the keyboard-driven apps did not compile.

 The boot code now snapshots the pointer into uefi-systab-addr before the page
 tables overwrite it, and every helper reads the snapshot. With no UEFI stub
 beneath us the snapshot is zero, and a helper that cannot reach firmware must
 SAY SO rather than jump into it.

 So this test asserts the boring thing, which is the whole point: the guest is
 still alive afterwards. It survived a crash for months precisely because
 nothing ever asserted that. -1 is "no key", and returning is the contract.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    let k = uefi-read-key
    in act
      print-line-uni ("scan=" & show k)
    end
    if k == -1 then print-line-uni "PASS no-firmware read returns -1 and returns"
    else print-line-uni "FAIL expected -1 with no UEFI firmware"
  end
